--TEST--
`Core\Fatal`'s two tiers are two slots: registering one leaves the other alone, a caught throw fires neither, and a second `onUncaughtThrow` replaces the first rather than queueing behind it — `rule:errors/on-limit` and `rule:errors/on-uncaught-throw`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two members are the same shape of registration over different slots
// (`Ctx::set_limit_handler`, `Ctx::set_uncaught_handler`), and a single slot
// shared between them passes every case written about one member alone. Here
// both are registered and only one failure happens, so a shared slot prints
// the wrong line or no line at all.
Core\Fatal::onLimit(fn (array<string> $report): void => {
    // § 1's tier fires for a resource limit and for nothing else. This program
    // stays inside every ceiling it has, so this line is unreachable — an
    // uncaught throw reaching it would mean the ladder had confused the two
    // failures the ladder exists to keep apart.
    echo "onLimit ran, for ", $report['limit'], "\n";
});

Core\Fatal::onUncaughtThrow(fn (Throwable $seen): void => {
    echo "first handler\n";
});

// § 2 gives the tier one handler and not a chain, for § 1's reason. The
// difference from its sibling's case is that this one can *watch* the
// replacement happen: a queue and a replacement print differently below,
// where tier 1's pair could only assert the silence of a ladder nobody fired.
Core\Fatal::onUncaughtThrow(fn (Throwable $seen): void => {
    echo "second handler: ", $seen->message, "\n";
});

// A throw that is caught never reaches the root, so no tier is climbed at all:
// § 2 is about the frames *running out*, not about a `Throwable` existing.
try {
    throw new LogicError("handled here");
} catch (LogicError $handled) {
    echo "caught: ", $handled->message, "\n";
}

echo "start\n";
throw new LogicError("boom");
--EXPECT--
caught: handled here
start
second handler: boom
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
{"level":"error","msg":"boom","fields":{"class":"LogicError"},"nodes":%s}
